WATCH OUT

  - Never ask Fred to repeat exhaustive manual checks; automate them.
    Fred, after W6: "This is not fun and violates a core principle of
    Tamlinux." Keep his checks brief and record the exact build he ran
    (its SHA-256).
  - Every code change bumps VERSION in the same commit.
  - Never link with -rdynamic or export lua_* symbols (libinput loads
    liblua 5.4 at runtime); make test enforces nm -D build/tam-pane.
  - Never toggle anything on Fred's active monitor; the nested window is
    where he tests.
  - Never ask Fred to press Ctrl+Alt+F-keys in the nested window: the
    host may switch his real VT.
  - Demos (sandbox/demo-<version> plus .lua): copy the latest one. They
    check that build/tam-pane reports VERSION, open the guide from a
    startup hook, and print PASS or FAIL themselves where they can.
    Write them with an editor, not shell heredocs: in W19 nested heredocs
    sharing one delimiter ran half a script in the agent's shell.
  - A Fred check needs no key pressed while Meta and a mouse button are
    held; use delays (W16).
  - The nested window is one monitor: tools/nested-input refuses two
    nested windows on purpose. Multi-monitor behaviour is checked by the
    headless tests and, by Fred's choice, on real hardware in P4.
  - third_party/lua/lstrlib.c carries one patch (lines marked
    "tam-pane patch"); re-apply it when updating Lua (UPSTREAM.md).
  - Every change updates every doc it makes wrong: README, ROADMAP.md,
    UPSTREAM.md, arch/, plans/, and test headers (Fred, W19).
